; kleene.tasm — the machine as a three-valued logic processor.
;
; Map the three trit states onto truth values:
;
;       -1 = false      0 = unknown      +1 = true
;
; Then MIN, MAX and NEG are not merely "like" AND, OR and NOT — they ARE Kleene's
; strong three-valued logic, exactly, on all twelve rows. That is the same logic
; SQL uses for NULL: unknown AND false is false (the answer is settled either
; way), but unknown AND true is unknown (it still depends).
;
; Inputs: two truth values. Outputs: NOT a, a AND b, a OR b, and a OR NOT a.
;
; That last one is the interesting output. In two-valued logic a OR NOT a is
; always true — the law of the excluded middle. Here, when a is unknown, it comes
; back UNKNOWN. The law fails, which is what makes this a genuinely three-valued
; logic rather than binary logic with a spare state lying around.

        IN
        STORE a
        IN
        STORE b

        LOAD  a
        NEG
        OUT                ; NOT a

        LOAD  a
        MIN   b
        OUT                ; a AND b

        LOAD  a
        MAX   b
        OUT                ; a OR b

        LOAD  a
        NEG
        STORE na
        LOAD  a
        MAX   na
        OUT                ; a OR NOT a  — not always true

        HALT

        .org  -6
a:      .word 0
b:      .word 0
na:     .word 0
